Diagnostics (CONFIG_JZ_DIAG_EPC_SLED, needs JZ_DIAG_LASTPANIC): on SoCs without a
reset-surviving register (T23) encode the last-panic record in the reset PC: after
the WDT is armed, spin in a 'b .; nop' loop at an uncached RAM address that encodes
the oops/lockup epc or reason+comm; the next boot reads it from CP0 ErrorEPC.
Also: skip RTC accesses when the RTC is absent, keep the hung task's comm.

diff -urN a/arch/mips/xburst/common/diag_lastpanic.c b/arch/mips/xburst/common/diag_lastpanic.c
--- a/arch/mips/xburst/common/diag_lastpanic.c	2026-10-31 20:30:42.719423282 +0100
+++ b/arch/mips/xburst/common/diag_lastpanic.c	2026-10-31 20:33:32.598052718 +0100
@@ -22,6 +22,26 @@
  *
  * /proc/diag_lastpanic: read = decoded previous record; write "oops",
  * "panic", "selftest" (store a known record, no reset; then reboot -f).
+ *
+ * Reset-PC channel (CONFIG_JZ_DIAG_EPC_SLED, for the T23, which has no RTC
+ * at 0x10003000 and no other reset-surviving register): the WDT reset
+ * stores the PC of the interrupted instruction in CP0 ErrorEPC, and the
+ * next boot prints it ("CPU0 RESET ERROR PC", cpu_probe_ingenic; proven by
+ * the __delay and user-space values seen after field resets). After the
+ * WDT is armed in jz_wdt_restart(), the CPU jumps to an uncached (kseg1)
+ * RAM address A where "b . ; nop" was written; ErrorEPC = A (the branch,
+ * also when the reset hits the delay slot). A encodes the record, phys:
+ *   0x0000000-0x03fffff  kernel oops,  epc = 0x80000000 | phys
+ *   0x0400000-0x07fffff  kernel lockup (epc of the interrupted context)
+ *   0x0800000-0x0ffffff  module oops,  epc = 0xc0000000 | (phys - 0x0800000)
+ *   0x1000000-0x17fffff  module lockup
+ *   0x1800000-0x1bffffc  other: Q = (phys - 0x1800000) >> 2,
+ *                        Q[19:16] reason, [15:14] uptime <10m <1h <24h >=24h,
+ *                        [13:7] [6:0] first two chars of comm (hung: the
+ *                        blocked task, else current)
+ * Everything stays below 0x1c00000, where the low ramoops window starts,
+ * and below U-Boot's malloc arena (U-Boot memsets 32 MB under its
+ * relocation address on every boot).
  */
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
